//@version=5
// ===========================================================================
// Comparison strategy — run this in TradingView, then compare its trade list
// with docs/tradingview/sma_cross_reliance_daily.md
//
// Everything here is chosen to remove reasons the two tools could differ for
// boring reasons, so that any difference that DOES show up means something:
//
//   SMA, not EMA   an EMA's value depends on how far back the chart's data
//                  begins, and TradingView has years more history than we do.
//                  An SMA only looks at the last N bars, so both tools must
//                  compute exactly the same number.
//   daily bars     hourly bars on NSE can be aligned differently by different
//                  providers, which shifts signals by a bar for no real reason.
//   no costs       fees and slippage are modelled differently everywhere. Set
//                  to zero on both sides, so this compares SIGNAL TIMING only.
//   stops far away 50% either side, so they never fire and cannot introduce
//                  a difference in how intra-candle stop hits are handled.
//   quantity 1     no position sizing to disagree about.
//
// Fills: both tools decide on the CLOSE of a bar and fill at the OPEN of the
// next one. In TradingView that is the default, and the settings block below
// keeps it that way.
// ===========================================================================

strategy("SMA 10/30 cross - engine comparison",
     overlay              = true,
     initial_capital      = 100000,
     default_qty_type     = strategy.fixed,
     default_qty_value    = 1,
     commission_type      = strategy.commission.percent,
     commission_value     = 0,
     slippage             = 0,
     pyramiding           = 0,
     process_orders_on_close = false,
     calc_on_every_tick   = false)

fast = ta.sma(close, 10)
slow = ta.sma(close, 30)

// Trade only inside the window our own data covers, so neither side takes a
// trade the other never had the chance to see.
startTime = timestamp("Asia/Kolkata", 2024, 4,  1, 0,  0)
endTime   = timestamp("Asia/Kolkata", 2026, 8, 21, 23, 59)
inWindow  = time >= startTime and time <= endTime

if inWindow and ta.crossover(fast, slow)
    strategy.entry("Long", strategy.long)

if inWindow and ta.crossunder(fast, slow)
    strategy.close("Long")

plot(fast, "SMA 10", color.new(color.blue,   0))
plot(slow, "SMA 30", color.new(color.orange, 0))
